@page "/"

<PageTitle>Overview — Argon2id.PasswordHasher WASM demo</PageTitle>

<h1>🔐 Argon2id.PasswordHasher — live in your browser</h1>

<p class="lede">
    This entire app &#8212; including the Argon2id hashing &#8212; runs
    <strong>inside your browser</strong>. There is no backend. The password
    never leaves your tab.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>This is a demo, not a starter template.</strong> Users live in
    page memory and disappear on reload. Hash internals are shown on screen
    so you can <em>see</em> what's happening &#8212; a real application
    would never display these values.
</div>

<h2>How is this possible?</h2>

<p>
    The <code>Argon2id.PasswordHasher</code> library is pure managed .NET. The
    underlying Argon2id implementation
    (<a href="https://github.com/kmaragon/Konscious.Security.Cryptography" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    Konscious</a>) uses only BCL primitives, so it compiles to WebAssembly
    just like any other .NET code. When you click Register, your browser
    spins up a WASM Argon2 computation and burns ~64 MiB of memory and a few
    hundred milliseconds doing the same work a server would do.
</p>

<h2>Try it</h2>

<ol class="steps">
    <li>
        <a href="register" class="step-link">
            <span class="step-number">1</span>
            <span>
                <strong>Register</strong> a username and password. The library
                hashes it with the default secure parameters
                (64 MiB / t=3 / p=1) and shows you the PHC string that would
                go into a database column.
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="login" class="step-link">
            <span class="step-number">2</span>
            <span>
                <strong>Log in</strong> with the same username and password.
                Verification re-runs Argon2id over the stored salt and
                compares the tag in constant time.
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="users" class="step-link">
            <span class="step-number">3</span>
            <span>
                <strong>Inspect</strong> the stored hashes &#8212; algorithm,
                version, work-factor parameters, salt, and tag &#8212; all
                packed into a single column-friendly value.
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Expect a wait.</strong> Argon2id is *designed* to be slow:
    that's what makes brute-force attacks expensive. In WebAssembly with
    production parameters, each hash takes a few seconds. That delay is
    the security feature.
</div>

<h2>What the library is doing</h2>

<pre><code class="lang-csharp">// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton(new Argon2idPasswordHasher());

// During registration
string stored = hasher.HashPassword(password);
// $argon2id$v=19$m=65536,t=3,p=1$&lt;salt&gt;$&lt;hash&gt;

// During login
bool ok = hasher.VerifyPassword(password, stored);

// Optional: upgrade old hashes when parameters change
if (hasher.NeedsRehash(stored))
    stored = hasher.HashPassword(password);
</code></pre>

<p>
    See <a href="https://github.com/systemslibrarian/argon2id-passwordhasher#readme"
    target="_blank" rel="noopener">the full README</a> for the design
    rationale, parameter-tuning guide, and the ASP.NET Core Identity adapter.
</p>
